# Public copy — agency name and licensed states

## Licensed states

The **only** public pages that name the current licensed states (today Nebraska, Kansas, Colorado, and Nevada) are:

- `licencias.html`
- `en/licenses.html`

Everywhere else (heroes, FAQs, CTAs, meta descriptions, landings, cost pages, homepage map copy, chatbot answers, email templates):

- Do **not** list those four states as “where we offer insurance.”
- Point readers to the licenses page (and NPN) instead.
- State *content* pages (`estados/`, `en/states/`) may still exist as local guides; do not frame them as a complete license roster.

When new states are added, update **only** the licenses pages (and the license map data), not site-wide boilerplate.

## “Julie” vs agency

On **webpage body, CTAs, FAQs, and meta descriptions**, use the agency — not the first name:

| Language | Use |
|----------|-----|
| English | **Mejor Vida Insurance** |
| Spanish | **Mejor Vida Seguros** |

Keep **Julie** only for:

- Author byline / JSON-LD `Julie Braunsroth`
- Footer/header NPN line (“Julie is a licensed insurance agent…”)
- Email `Julie@mejorvidainsurance.com`
- About-the-founder page (`about-julie.html` / `en/about-julie.html`) as a biography
- Chat widget name / `aria-label` for Julie’s assistant

Examples:

```html
<!-- BAD -->
<p>Julie compares options in Nebraska, Kansas, Colorado, and Nevada.</p>
<p>Call Julie at 402-440-5438.</p>

<!-- GOOD -->
<p>Mejor Vida Insurance compares options based on your age, health, and state.</p>
<p>Call Mejor Vida Insurance at 402-440-5438.</p>
<p>Current licenses are on the <a href="licenses.html">licenses</a> page.</p>
```
